2026-09-23T07:02:26.8941521Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8941749Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8941822Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8941924Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942002Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942095Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942173Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942276Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942360Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942448Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942526Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942602Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942690Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942790Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8942875Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943061Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943158Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943235Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943314Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943484Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943580Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943658Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943749Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943828Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8943972Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944138Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944217Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944311Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944407Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944497Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944594Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944671Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944776Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8944931Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8945010Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8945083Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8945952Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T07:02:26.8946040Z     
